-  <p node="[object Object]" class="" data-ag-id="ag-n-201">This perfectly illustrates the risk! If I had bypassed the framework and spawned the subagent in your live directory, its final instruction (<code node="[object Object]" class="whitespace-pre-wrap" data-ag-id="ag-n-202" style="word-break: break-word;">git add -A; git commit -m "isolated patch"</code>) would have accidentally swept up your uncommitted changes to <code node="[object Object]" class="whitespace-pre-wrap" data-ag-id="ag-n-203" style="word-break: break-word;">memory_extractor.py</code>, bundled them into the patch, and permanently entangled your unrelated work with the synchronization fix.</p>
-  <p node="[object Object]" class="" data-ag-id="ag-n-204">Because you have uncommitted work, we have two safe options to proceed:</p>
-  <ol data-ag-id="ag-n-205">
-  <li node="[object Object]" data-ag-id="ag-n-206"><strong data-ag-id="ag-n-207">Option A (The Intended Way):</strong> Set the active workspace to <code node="[object Object]" class="whitespace-pre-wrap" data-ag-id="ag-n-208" style="word-break: break-word;">C:\Users\nferr\OneDrive\Documents\Projects\cortex</code> in the Antigravity UI. The framework will automatically create a true isolated clone from your last commit, completely ignoring your unstaged changes in <code node="[object Object]" class="whitespace-pre-wrap" data-ag-id="ag-n-209" style="word-break: break-word;">memory_extractor.py</code>.</li>
